## PART 3 — THE PROCESS (how we actually build it)

### Phase 0 — Foundation (before features)

1. Define the product in one sentence: who, what, why.
2. List the 3-5 core user jobs (not features — jobs the user needs done).
3. Sketch the 5-10 screens that cover those jobs. Paper or Figma, doesn't matter.
4. Build the design system (above) as code: `tokens.ts`, base components, theme provider.

### Phase 1 — Skeleton flows

Build the *happy path* of each core job end-to-end. A user can complete the job, even if the UI is plain. No dead ends, no "coming soon" buttons. If a feature isn't done, the button doesn't exist.

### Phase 2 — Real data layer

Wire everything to Supabase. Real auth, real CRUD, real RLS policies. No mock data left in. This is where most AI-built apps fake it — we won't.

### Phase 3 — First polish pass (visual)

Apply the design system everywhere. Replace placeholder text with real micro-copy. Tighten spacing. Add the motion language. This is where it starts looking like a real product.

### Phase 4 — Edge case pass

For every screen, ask:
- What if there's no data? (empty state with a clear next action)
- What if it's loading? (skeleton matching the layout, not a spinner)
- What if it errors? (helpful message, retry button, no crash)
- What if there's a lot of data? (pagination/virtualization, not a 5000-item DOM)
- What if the user is on mobile? (real responsive, not just "it fits")
- What if the user is offline / slow? (optimistic UI, retry queues)

### Phase 5 — Interaction polish pass

- Every clickable thing has hover, active, focus states
- Every form validates on blur, shows errors clearly, disables submit until valid
- Transitions between states are animated
- Keyboard navigation works everywhere (Tab order, Escape closes modals, Enter submits)
- Focus rings are visible and consistent

### Phase 6 — Accessibility pass

- Run axe/Lighthouse. Fix everything.
- Screen-reader test the core flows.
- Color contrast audit on every text/bg combination.
- All images have alt text. All buttons have accessible names.
- `prefers-reduced-motion` and `prefers-color-scheme` respected.

### Phase 7 — Performance pass

- Lighthouse score 90+ on all four metrics.
- Code-split routes. Lazy-load heavy components.
- Images: WebP/AVIF, proper sizing, lazy-load below the fold.
- Bundle size budget (e.g. < 200KB JS initial).
- LCP < 2.5s on a cold load.

### Phase 8 — Micro-copy pass

This is huge and underrated. Top products have *writing* that's:
- Specific, not generic ("Your password must be 8+ characters" not "Invalid input")
- Reassuring in errors ("Couldn't load your projects. Try again?" not "Error 500")
- Active voice, present tense, second person
- No exclamation marks. No "please." No jargon.

### Phase 9 — Final detail pass

- Pixel-check alignment on every screen
- Verify hover states don't cause layout shift
- Check that long text doesn't break layouts
- Test with real-looking data, not "test test test"
- Proofread every string

